.TH CHTTPSVR_SUBROUTER 3 "2026-08-03" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_subrouter \- create a sub-router with a path prefix
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

chttpsvr_router *chttpsvr_subrouter(chttpsvr srv, const char *prefix);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new router under the given path prefix (which must begin with
\&'/') and attaches it to the server.  Routes registered on the sub-router
are matched against the portion of the path following the prefix.

The prefix must not contain consecutive slashes (e.g. "//api" or
"/a//b").  Such prefixes can never match a valid HTTP path and would
silently create an unreachable router; chttpsvr_subrouter rejects them
by returning NULL.

A trailing slash in prefix is stripped automatically: "/api/v1/" and
"/api/v1" are therefore equivalent and produce identical routing
behaviour.

The returned pointer is owned by the server and must not be freed by
the caller.  Every chttpsvr_router_on, chttpsvr_router_on_stream, and
chttpsvr_router_use call against the returned router validates that
this server has not been destroyed (including concurrently, from
another thread) before touching it, so it is safe to hold the returned
pointer across a chttpsvr_stop/chttpsvr_start restart, and a
registration call racing a concurrent chttpsvr_destroy of srv simply
returns ccol_invalid_args rather than touching freed memory.

This function is thread-safe and may be called before or after
chttpsvr_start is running.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The "/" prefix is a special case.  The trailing-slash strip loop runs
only when the prefix length exceeds one character, so "/" is retained
as-is with prefix_len = 1.  A "/" sub-router matches only the exact
request path "/"; any other path, including one beginning with a
second slash (e.g. "//foo"), does NOT match and receives 404.

Use prefix "/" only when you intend to match only the exact root path
"/".  To catch all requests regardless of path, register routes directly
on the server with chttpsvr_register_handler or chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler instead.

Root-router shadowing: routes registered directly on the server via
chttpsvr_register_handler or chttpsvr_register_streaming_handler (i.e.
on the root router) are always evaluated before any sub-router.  A
root-level route whose path AND method both match a request wins
outright, shadowing a same-path sub-router route completely.  A
root-level route that matches the same path but a different method
does not shadow the sub-router: every other root-level route is still
tried for a same-path, same-method match first, and only once the
whole root router has been exhausted does matching fall through to
the sub-router, where a route whose own method matches the request is
served normally (the same 405-vs-404 priority two same-router routes
registered under different methods already follow; see
chttpsvr_register_handler(3)).  To avoid unintentional shadowing, do
not register root-level routes whose paths overlap with a sub-router's
prefix and pattern for the same method.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Pointer to the new chttpsvr_router on success, or NULL when srv is
CHTTPSVR_INVALID or a stale/already-destroyed handle, prefix is NULL,
prefix does not start with '/', prefix contains consecutive slashes,
or on OOM.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_router_on (3),
.BR chttpsvr_router_use (3)
